course: dictionary
lesson: 10789
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. The words around a word, its context, show you which meaning the writer means. «Vampire» is a good example: it can mean a creature from legends, a real animal, or a word for someone who drains your energy.
explain: «Vampire bats can walk, run and hop on all fours, creeping up on a sleeping animal on foot.» The clues are creeping, sleeping animal and on foot, so this vampire is a real animal. A legend would not need these clues. Always ask: which meaning fits the clues?
explain: «Films show bats tangling in hair and vampires biting people.» Here vampires are the made-up creatures of stories and films, not real animals. When you meet a word you know, check whether this meaning is the one the text needs.
explain: In everyday talk, someone might say «energy vampire» about a person who makes you feel tired and drained. This is an informal meaning. It does not describe a real animal or a dead person at all.
fact: In stories and legends, a vampire is a dead person who rises at night and drinks the blood of living people. This is made up.
fact: A vampire bat is a real small bat from Central and South America that feeds on the blood of animals.
fact: Real vampire bats hardly ever bite people. They make a tiny cut and lap up a little blood.
fact: Vampire bat spit stops blood going thick and sticky, so the cut keeps flowing. Scientists have studied it to make new medicines.
fact: Folk tales say vampires fear garlic, sunlight and crosses, and have no reflection. These are made-up beliefs, not facts about real bats.
exercise: «Films show bats tangling in hair and vampires biting people.» In this sentence, what does vampires mean? | A small real bat from South America | *Made-up creatures from stories and films | A tired person who needs a nap | A kind of Halloween sweet
exercise: «Vampire bats can walk, run and hop on all fours, creeping up on a sleeping animal on foot.» What is a vampire bat in this sentence? | A dead person who rises at night | A made-up monster from a film | *A real small bat that feeds on animal blood | A person who drains your energy
exercise: Mum says, «Ben is an energy vampire, he drains my time and money.» What does energy vampire mean? | A person who loves Halloween costumes | *Someone who drains your energy, time or money | A dark, scary sign | A bat that lives in caves
test: Which fact is true about real vampire bats? | They fear garlic and sunlight | *They hardly ever bite people | They have no reflection | They rise from the grave at night
test: «Vampire bat spit stops blood going thick and sticky, so the cut keeps flowing.» Why have scientists studied it? | To find out if vampires are real | *To make new medicines | To make Halloween costumes | To scare the bats away
exam: In «Her brother is an energy vampire, he sucks all the fun out of every game», vampire means | *A person who drains your energy or fun | A real bat from South America | A dark, scary sign | A dead person who rises at night
